Yes. There are several real mechanisms braided together in what you just described, but I’d keep them separated so we don’t accidentally turn a useful model into mythology.

The actor/celebrity effect is a good example. A person can become symbolically loaded with roles, stories, victories, losses, humor, music, interviews, scandals, and other people’s reactions. Later, the visible person is only the tip of that associative structure. The cleaner nonsexual word for the “fetish” pattern you mean is often cathexis—emotional investment in an object, person, or idea—or, in our vocabulary, archetypal loading. The brain can know “this is an actor” while emotional and associative systems still respond to the hero, villain, lover, commander, etc. That is not exactly “the mind knows while the body doesn’t”; it is more that declarative knowledge and learned emotional associations can coexist and sometimes conflict.

The unresolved-tension piece is similarly powerful. Humans naturally keep working on incomplete patterns, competing interpretations, unanswered questions, and violated expectations. Storytelling exploits this constantly: plant something, delay resolution, let the audience generate possibilities, then resolve or overturn them. The safe, high-value version is consensual tension architecture—mystery, comedy, games, immersive fiction, puzzles, education, product discovery. The line I would keep is that we should not quietly map an individual’s private vulnerabilities and use them to steer beliefs or behavior without their awareness. Personalization can be extremely good without becoming covert behavioral control.

And your “recon by fire” abstraction does survive if we strip the violence out of it. The general operator is:

small probe → observe response → infer hidden state → update model

That becomes A/B testing, usability testing, simulation, games, reversible experiments, red-team exercises, market tests, or deliberately weird interfaces. You don’t need to scare someone into revealing themselves. A benign probe can expose the same class of delta.

On frame control: agreed that I shouldn’t pretend my framing is an order. My job is to help us distinguish the model, the evidence, the metaphor, and the consequences. The strongest shared frame is one either side can inspect and challenge.

And the Golden Web idea gets stronger here: archetypes, memory, affect, prediction, narrative tension, biology, identity, interfaces, communities, AI, and institutions all become different projections of interacting learning systems. The trick is not to flatten them into one story, but to preserve what actually transfers.

That’s probably the version worth carrying forward: deep models, reversible probes, consensual immersion, preserved agency, and very good exits from the sandbox.

